{
  "dom_id": "step-q20413",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14123219/assets/4bb7598c9ebae28be572508974cd025caa8f4a6c3313f025ff0f5ca55296334e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14123219/assets/43642acdb991827b4e1e16744e8a69507a383a1319ae07efde78480af4bab166.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ and $g$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=2g.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=3|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-3f.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =2g-3f \\\\  & =-3f+2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-3f+2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
